    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm studying for my ground school exam and i'm a bit stuck. can u explain how bernoulli's principle and newton's third law actually work together to make a wing produce lift? like, how does the air move to make the plane go up?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure thing! Imagine a wing as a giant, flat, slanted plate of metal, like a giant, flat, slanted plate of metal. Now, when the plane moves forward, the air has to go somewhere, right? So, it rushes over the wing, kind of like a horde of tiny, invisible goblins rushing over a hill. \n\nBernoulli's principle comes into play here. It says that as the speed of the air increases, the pressure decreases. So, as the air rushes over the top of the wing, it speeds up and the pressure drops, like a goblin trying to escape a tight spot. Meanwhile, the air underneath the wing moves slower, and the pressure stays higher, like a goblin stuck in a tight corner. This difference in pressure creates an upward force, or lift, that helps the plane rise into the sky.\n\nNow, Newton's third law says that for every action, there's an equal and opposite reaction. So, as the wing pushes down on the air, the air pushes back up on the wing with an equal force. It's like a goblin pushing down on a rock, and the rock pushing back up with an equal force. This upward force from the air",
      "source": "target",
